{

!  Nearest-neighbour fragmentation of a covalent network solid, two
!  shells deep. This is the test that the shell counting is right: at
!  level 2 the fragment reaches past the first neighbours to their
!  neighbours, and only then caps.
!
!     group-1-H12O4Si5   Si centre + 4 O + 4 Si, capped with 12 H
!     group-2-H6O7Si2    O  centre + 2 Si + 6 O, capped with  6 H
!
!  Those counts are forced by quartz connectivity -- silicon is
!  4-coordinate and oxygen 2-coordinate:
!
!     Si centre: 4 O in shell 1; each O carries 1 further Si, giving 4
!     Si in shell 2; each of those has 3 remaining bonds, so 12 caps.
!
!     O centre: 2 Si in shell 1; each Si carries 3 further O, giving 6
!     O in shell 2; each of those has 1 remaining bond, so 6 caps.
!
!  Getting the loop bound wrong by one shell changes these formulae, so
!  the file names alone pin the counting down. The bug this guards
!  against is real: do_NN_defragment used to leave its loop through an
!  "nc_atoms = nc_atoms + 1; exit" branch that stopped a shell early.
!
!  Davidson (2023) chapter 5 finds level 2 no better than level 1, and
!  suggests level 2 fragments over-represent an atom's interaction with
!  itself. Again, not a statement about this code.

   name= quartz

   output_style_options= { real_precision= 4 }

   basis_name= STO-3G

   charge= 0
   multiplicity= 1

   CIF= { file_name= quartz_init.cif }
   process_CIF

   crystal= {
      xray_data= {
         NN_level= 2
         use_NN_capping= TRUE
      }
   }

   put_atom_groups

}
